  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>In the given structure, ring one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mfenced></math> has one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math>-atom and is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn></math> membered ring, thus is called pyranose ring, whereas ring <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>II</mi></mfenced></math> has one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math>-atom and is a five membered ring, thus is called furanose.</p><p>The disaccharide is linked by a glycosidic bond between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></math> of glucose (in the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#945;</mi></math> -position, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#945;</mi></math> linkage) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;of fructose (in the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#946;</mi></math>-position, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#946;</mi></math>-linkage).</p>",
